From 504aa71299d1bbcc33fb4420fa7abdf0ef760c24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nathan Singh <nathan.singh@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 15:51:51 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Native: a Windows build (32-bit MinGW)

The native port also builds as a 32-bit Windows program, in MSYS2's
MINGW32 shell, with the same Makefile (OS=Windows_NT): tbs-native.exe,
needing only system DLLs (and SDL3.dll for the window).

- PE symbols: 32-bit Windows C names start with an underscore. The
  generated and converted assembly gets it after assembling (objcopy
  --prefix-symbols=_); far_aliases.py and overlay_object.py add it
  (SYMBOL_PREFIX); ASM_LINKED_NAME takes the compiler's
  __USER_LABEL_PREFIX__. arm2host_s.py --coff drops .type and .size and
  keeps only .section's name.
- Layout: -mno-ms-bitfields, so structs match the GBA's. The image is
  linked at 0x08000000 (the ROM range, as Linux's 0x08048000), without
  relocation or DEP; the regions are mapped with VirtualAlloc, before the
  platform loads any library.
- The host stack and heap are below EWRAM on Windows: the stack pointer
  the frame wait reads is now a fixed address above every region (on
  both systems), and the DMA null guard is the lowest host address.
- Overlays: each is linked with a small script into one .text, .data
  (data and read-only data) and .bss, and objcopy --add-symbol marks each
  section's bounds; the table holds them (no ELF __start_/__stop_), and
  Native_IsOverlayCode looks them up. Same on ELF.
- The window links SDL3 delay-loaded (README): loaded at start-up, it and
  the system libraries it pulls in left a mapped view over EWRAM's address.
  A failed mapping now says what holds the address.
- Smoke script paths take either slash; a crash prints its address;
  soft reset restarts through _execv.

Verified: on Windows, fight.txt runs to the win and its nine shots are
byte-identical to the Linux build's; the Linux build with this change
gives the same shots again. The Windows window boots into the lobby.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
